insert into public.flashcards (id, cert, deck, domain, objective, front, back) values
('sa-f-500','awssaa','core',4,'4.1',$$What is the cost and durability trade-off of S3 One Zone-IA versus Standard-IA?$$,$$One Zone-IA stores data in a single Availability Zone instead of three-plus, costing about 20% less than Standard-IA, but the data is lost if that AZ is destroyed - appropriate only for easily recreatable data or a secondary copy of data that already exists elsewhere.$$),
('sa-f-501','awssaa','core',4,'4.1',$$When does S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval save money over S3 Standard-IA?$$,$$For data accessed roughly once a quarter or less that still needs millisecond retrieval - Glacier Instant Retrieval charges a lower storage price than Standard-IA in exchange for a higher retrieval fee, so it wins economically once access is rare enough.$$),
('sa-f-502','awssaa','core',4,'4.1',$$What are the three retrieval speed tiers within S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval, and what do they cost?$$,$$Expedited (1-5 minutes, highest per-GB retrieval price), Standard (3-5 hours, moderate price), and Bulk (5-12 hours, the cheapest retrieval option) - choose the tier based on how urgently the archived data is needed.$$),
('sa-f-503','awssaa','core',4,'4.1',$$What are S3's minimum storage duration charges, and which classes have them?$$,$$Standard-IA and One Zone-IA bill for a minimum of 30 days per object, Glacier Flexible Retrieval for 90 days, and Glacier Deep Archive for 180 days - deleting or transitioning an object earlier still bills for the remaining minimum period.$$),
('sa-f-504','awssaa','core',4,'4.1',$$Why can transitioning an object to S3 Standard-IA too early actually cost more, not less?$$,$$S3 requires an object to have already been stored for at least 30 days before it can transition into Standard-IA or One Zone-IA; combined with the 30-day minimum storage charge on the destination class, transitioning very young or short-lived objects adds cost instead of saving it.$$),
('sa-f-505','awssaa','core',4,'4.1',$$What does an S3 Lifecycle rule to abort incomplete multipart uploads do?$$,$$It automatically deletes the parts of a multipart upload that was never completed - due to a failed or abandoned upload - after a set number of days, so you stop paying storage cost for orphaned upload parts that nothing ever finishes or cleans up.$$),
('sa-f-506','awssaa','core',4,'4.1',$$What actually drives an S3 bill, beyond just GB stored per month?$$,$$Storage volume and duration, request charges (PUT/COPY/POST/LIST cost more per request than GET/SELECT), data retrieval fees on IA and Glacier classes, and data transfer OUT to the internet - data transfer IN to S3 is always free.$$),
('sa-f-507','awssaa','core',4,'4.1',$$How does choosing gp3 over gp2 for an EBS volume reduce cost, not just change performance?$$,$$gp3's baseline 3,000 IOPS and 125 MB/s throughput are included in a lower per-GB price than gp2, and gp2's cost rises simply because its IOPS scale (and are capped) with volume size - so gp3 is typically cheaper for the same or better baseline performance, independent of any extra performance you might provision.$$),
('sa-f-508','awssaa','core',4,'4.1',$$How are EBS snapshots billed, and what keeps their storage cost from growing unchecked?$$,$$Each snapshot after the first stores only the blocks that changed since the previous snapshot, not a full copy, which already limits cost; Amazon Data Lifecycle Manager (DLM) further controls cost by automating snapshot creation and deleting old snapshots on a retention schedule so they don't accumulate indefinitely.$$),
('sa-f-509','awssaa','core',4,'4.1',$$What does AWS S3 Storage Lens provide for cost optimization?$$,$$Organization-wide visibility into storage usage and activity metrics - including non-current object versions and incomplete multipart uploads - across all accounts and buckets, surfacing exactly where storage spend is being wasted.$$),
('sa-f-510','awssaa','core',4,'4.1',$$How does moving infrequently used files to EFS-IA reduce cost, and what automates it?$$,$$EFS Infrequent Access storage costs a small fraction of EFS Standard per GB for files not actively read or written; EFS Lifecycle Management automatically moves files that haven't been accessed for a configured number of days into EFS-IA with no application changes required.$$),
('sa-a-160','awssaa','acronym',4,'4.1',$$S3$$,$$Simple Storage Service - AWS's object storage service, whose storage-class pricing tiers (Standard, IA, Glacier) are the main lever for optimizing storage cost.$$),
('sa-a-161','awssaa','acronym',4,'4.1',$$RRS$$,$$Reduced Redundancy Storage - a legacy, deprecated S3 storage class that cost less than Standard by storing data with lower durability (99.99% versus eleven nines); current storage classes have replaced its use case.$$),
('sa-y-080','awssaa','feynman',4,'4.1',$$Explain how you'd design an S3 Lifecycle policy for log files that are hit constantly for a week, occasionally for a month, and essentially never again after that - and why the 30-day minimum storage rule matters to that design.$$,$$Model answer: Start the objects in S3 Standard where they land, since access is heavy in the first week and Standard has no minimum duration or retrieval fee. After about 30 days - satisfying the minimum-storage-duration rule that applies to IA classes - transition them to Standard-IA, where they'll sit through the "occasional access" month at a lower storage price with a small per-GB retrieval fee that's fine given the low read volume. After roughly 90 days of near-zero access, transition again to Glacier Flexible Retrieval (or Deep Archive if compliance requires years of retention), accepting slower retrieval in exchange for the lowest storage price. Doing an early jump straight to Glacier at day 2 would trigger both the loss of convenient access during the still-busy first month and the minimum-duration billing penalty if anything needed deleting or moving again soon after.$$),
('sa-y-081','awssaa','feynman',4,'4.1',$$Explain why an S3 Lifecycle rule to abort incomplete multipart uploads is often described as "free money" for cost optimization.$$,$$Model answer: A multipart upload that never completes - because a client crashed, a network connection dropped, or an application bug stopped short of the final CompleteMultipartUpload call - leaves its uploaded parts sitting in the bucket. Those parts are billed as normal storage even though no object was ever created and nothing can read or use them; without a cleanup rule, they simply accumulate forever as pure waste. Since there's no legitimate use for an incomplete upload's parts, aborting them after a short window (a day or a few days) recovers that storage cost with zero downside - there's no trade-off to weigh, which is why it's close to a free cost win.$$),
('sa-f-511','awssaa','core',4,'4.2',$$Does creating an EC2 On-Demand Capacity Reservation reduce your compute cost by itself?$$,$$No - a Capacity Reservation guarantees capacity at full On-Demand pricing whether or not you use it. The bill only drops if you also apply a Savings Plan (or a Reserved Instance) to the reserved capacity; the reservation and the discount are two separate, independently-purchased things.$$),
('sa-f-512','awssaa','core',4,'4.2',$$How do the maximum discounts of an EC2 Instance Savings Plan and a Compute Savings Plan compare?$$,$$EC2 Instance Savings Plans can save up to about 72% by committing to a specific instance family and region; Compute Savings Plans cap out lower, around 66%, in exchange for applying flexibly across instance families, regions, and even Lambda/Fargate usage.$$),
('sa-f-513','awssaa','core',4,'4.2',$$How can a Spot Fleet or Auto Scaling group reduce the risk of Spot interruptions while still capturing Spot savings?$$,$$By diversifying across many instance types, sizes, and Availability Zones and using an allocation strategy like capacity-optimized, which launches from the pools AWS reports as least likely to be reclaimed soon - spreading risk instead of depending on one instance type in one pool.$$),
('sa-f-514','awssaa','core',4,'4.2',$$What two factors determine an AWS Lambda invocation's cost, and why might raising a function's memory setting lower total cost?$$,$$Lambda bills per request plus GB-seconds (memory allocated times execution duration). More memory also proportionally increases CPU, so a function can finish enough faster that the GB-seconds actually charged goes down even though the per-second rate went up - AWS Compute Optimizer can recommend the memory setting that minimizes total cost for a given function.$$),
('sa-f-515','awssaa','core',4,'4.2',$$How does billing differ between an EC2 Dedicated Host and a Dedicated Instance?$$,$$A Dedicated Host is billed per physical host, giving visibility into and control over specific sockets and cores - needed to bring existing per-socket/per-core software licenses (BYOL); a Dedicated Instance is billed per instance-hour like a normal EC2 instance plus a flat per-region dedicated-tenancy fee, without host-level visibility.$$),
('sa-a-162','awssaa','acronym',4,'4.2',$$SP$$,$$Savings Plans - a commitment to a consistent $/hour of compute spend for 1 or 3 years in exchange for a discount versus On-Demand pricing.$$),
('sa-a-163','awssaa','acronym',4,'4.2',$$ODCR$$,$$On-Demand Capacity Reservation - reserves EC2 capacity in a specific instance type and Availability Zone with no term commitment and no discount by itself.$$),
('sa-a-164','awssaa','acronym',4,'4.2',$$TCO$$,$$Total Cost of Ownership - the full cost comparison (compute, storage, staffing, facilities) AWS's Pricing Calculator and TCO tools use to weigh running workloads on AWS against on-premises.$$),
('sa-y-082','awssaa','feynman',4,'4.2',$$Explain the counterintuitive case where increasing an AWS Lambda function's memory allocation actually lowers its total cost.$$,$$Model answer: Lambda's price per GB-second goes up as you allocate more memory, so it looks like more memory should always cost more. But memory allocation also scales CPU power proportionally in Lambda, so a CPU-bound function given more memory can finish in a fraction of the time. Total cost is (memory in GB) x (duration in seconds) x (price per GB-second) x number of invocations - if doubling the memory more than halves the duration, the GB-seconds actually billed goes down even though the per-second price went up, so the total bill drops. This is why AWS Compute Optimizer specifically profiles Lambda functions and can recommend a HIGHER memory setting purely to save money, not just to go faster.$$),
('sa-y-083','awssaa','feynman',4,'4.2',$$Explain the practical risk trade-off between committing to a Savings Plan and buying a Reserved Instance for a workload whose future size is uncertain.$$,$$Model answer: A Savings Plan is use-it-or-lose-it - you're billed the committed hourly amount for the full 1- or 3-year term regardless of whether your usage ever reaches it, and there's no way to exit early or resell the commitment if the workload shrinks or gets decommissioned. A Standard Reserved Instance carries the same use-it-or-lose-it billing, but it can be listed and sold on the RI Marketplace if it's no longer needed, recovering some value; a Convertible RI can't be resold but can be exchanged for a different instance family, size, OS, or tenancy as needs change. So the practical choice is about how much certainty you have: rock-solid steady usage favors either option for the discount, but real uncertainty about future needs favors an RI (ideally Convertible) over a Savings Plan, purely for the exit options it preserves.$$),
('sa-f-516','awssaa','core',4,'4.3',$$How is Aurora Serverless v2 capacity billed?$$,$$Per Aurora Capacity Unit-hour (ACU-hour), where each ACU is roughly 2 GiB of memory with matching CPU and networking; the cluster scales continuously in fine-grained increments within an admin-set minimum and maximum ACU range, and you pay only for the ACUs actually in use at each moment.$$),
('sa-f-517','awssaa','core',4,'4.3',$$How do RDS Reserved Instances reduce database cost, and what do they commit you to?$$,$$Like EC2 RIs, you commit to a specific DB instance class, engine, and region for a 1- or 3-year term in exchange for a significant discount versus On-Demand hourly pricing - a good fit for a database whose size and engine are stable and known well in advance.$$),
('sa-f-518','awssaa','core',4,'4.3',$$Why does enabling RDS Multi-AZ roughly double the instance-hour cost of a database?$$,$$The synchronous standby in the second Availability Zone is a full, separately billed instance of the same class as the primary - you're paying for two running instances instead of one, even though only the primary serves traffic day to day.$$),
('sa-f-519','awssaa','core',4,'4.3',$$How can stopping an RDS instance reduce cost for a development or test database?$$,$$A stopped RDS instance isn't billed for compute (instance-hours), only for its allocated storage - useful for dev/test databases that only need to run during business hours. AWS automatically restarts an instance left stopped for 7 days, so long-term shutdowns need an automated stop/start schedule.$$),
('sa-f-520','awssaa','core',4,'4.3',$$When is DynamoDB On-Demand capacity cheaper than Provisioned capacity, and when is it the reverse?$$,$$On-Demand charges a per-request rate that is several times higher per unit than Provisioned throughput, so at steady, predictable, high-volume traffic, Provisioned (especially with Reserved Capacity) costs less; at low, spiky, or unpredictable traffic, On-Demand wins because you never pay for provisioned capacity that sits idle.$$),
('sa-f-521','awssaa','core',4,'4.3',$$What is DynamoDB Reserved Capacity?$$,$$A 1- or 3-year upfront commitment to a fixed amount of provisioned read and write capacity at a discounted hourly rate versus standard Provisioned pricing - it only applies to Provisioned-mode tables, not On-Demand tables.$$),
('sa-f-522','awssaa','core',4,'4.3',$$How do DynamoDB Auto Scaling and Reserved Capacity work together to control cost?$$,$$Reserved Capacity buys a cheap, committed floor of provisioned throughput for a table's steady baseline load; Auto Scaling then adjusts capacity above that floor to track a target utilization as traffic varies, so you get the discount on the predictable part of the load without paying peak-provisioned rates around the clock.$$),
('sa-f-523','awssaa','core',4,'4.3',$$What is the DynamoDB Standard-IA table class, and when should you use it?$$,$$An alternate DynamoDB table class with lower per-GB storage pricing but higher per-request throughput pricing than the DynamoDB Standard table class - it reduces total cost for tables where storage, not request throughput, dominates the bill, such as large tables holding infrequently accessed data.$$),
('sa-f-524','awssaa','core',4,'4.3',$$How does enabling DynamoDB TTL (Time to Live) reduce cost?$$,$$TTL automatically deletes items past their expiration timestamp for free, in the background, shrinking the table's stored data volume (lowering storage cost) without consuming write capacity units the way an application-driven bulk delete would.$$),
('sa-f-525','awssaa','core',4,'4.3',$$What ongoing cost should you weigh before adopting Aurora Global Database?$$,$$Global Database keeps replicated storage in every secondary region plus at least one running reader instance per secondary region, all billed continuously - worth it only when the cross-region read-latency or disaster-recovery benefit is actually needed, not adopted by default for a single-region application.$$),
('sa-f-526','awssaa','core',4,'4.3',$$How does Aurora Serverless v2 reduce wasted spend compared to Aurora Serverless v1?$$,$$v1 scales in discrete capacity steps and can briefly pause connections during a scaling event, so it tends to over-provision between steps; v2 scales continuously in much finer-grained increments with no connection-dropping pause, tracking actual load more precisely and reducing the ACU-hours you pay for but don't use.$$),
('sa-f-527','awssaa','core',4,'4.3',$$What RDS backup storage is free, and when does backup storage start costing money?$$,$$RDS gives you backup storage free up to the size of your total database storage; once accumulated automated and manual snapshots exceed that allowance (or the source database is deleted while snapshots are retained), the excess backup storage is billed per GB-month - a reason to prune old manual snapshots you no longer need.$$),
('sa-f-528','awssaa','core',4,'4.3',$$Why can idle RDS read replicas be a hidden cost problem?$$,$$Each read replica is a fully separate, continuously billed running instance regardless of how much read traffic it actually serves - a replica added for a traffic spike or a since-retired reporting job keeps costing money until someone notices and deletes or downsizes it.$$),
('sa-f-529','awssaa','core',4,'4.3',$$What is a cost-efficient way to size database capacity commitments for a workload with a steady baseline plus occasional bursts?$$,$$Commit to Reserved Instances (RDS) or Reserved Capacity (DynamoDB) sized to the steady baseline load to capture the discount, and let On-Demand capacity - or DynamoDB's On-Demand mode / Auto Scaling - absorb the burst above that baseline, rather than either committing to peak capacity or paying full On-Demand rates for the whole load.$$),
('sa-a-165','awssaa','acronym',4,'4.3',$$ACU$$,$$Aurora Capacity Unit - the billing and scaling unit for Aurora Serverless v2, roughly 2 GiB of memory with matching CPU and network capacity.$$),
('sa-a-166','awssaa','acronym',4,'4.3',$$WCU$$,$$Write Capacity Unit - the DynamoDB provisioned-throughput billing unit for writes; one WCU covers one write per second of up to 1 KB.$$),
('sa-a-167','awssaa','acronym',4,'4.3',$$RCU$$,$$Read Capacity Unit - the DynamoDB provisioned-throughput billing unit for reads; one RCU covers one strongly consistent read per second of up to 4 KB (or two eventually consistent reads of the same size).$$),
('sa-a-168','awssaa','acronym',4,'4.3',$$TTL$$,$$Time to Live - a DynamoDB feature that automatically deletes items past a set expiration timestamp, reducing stored data volume at no extra write-capacity cost.$$),
('sa-y-084','awssaa','feynman',4,'4.3',$$Explain how you'd decide between DynamoDB On-Demand and Provisioned-with-Reserved-Capacity for two different tables: one serving a viral app with unpredictable traffic, and one serving a stable internal tool with flat, well-known traffic.$$,$$Model answer: The deciding factor is how well you can predict the load and how steady it is. The viral app's traffic could 10x overnight or vanish just as fast - Provisioned capacity would force a constant guessing game, either throttling on unexpected spikes or paying for headroom that sits idle most of the time. On-Demand charges per request with no capacity planning at all, so it costs a bit more per request but perfectly tracks unpredictable demand with zero waste and zero throttling risk. The internal tool's traffic barely changes month to month, so its capacity needs are essentially known in advance - buying Reserved Capacity locks in that steady throughput at a meaningfully lower per-unit rate than either On-Demand or standard Provisioned pricing, and Auto Scaling can still absorb the occasional small variation above that reserved floor. In short: predictable and steady favors committing (Reserved Capacity), unpredictable and spiky favors paying only for what happens (On-Demand).$$),
('sa-y-085','awssaa','feynman',4,'4.3',$$Explain why RDS Multi-AZ effectively doubles compute cost, why many production databases still use it anyway, and how that cost/benefit differs from adding a read replica.$$,$$Model answer: Multi-AZ's standby is a full, idle-until-needed copy of the primary instance, billed exactly like a second production instance around the clock - you're paying for redundancy you hope never activates. Despite that, production databases pay it because the alternative (a single instance) means any AZ outage or hardware failure takes the database down until manual intervention; the automatic failover Multi-AZ provides is usually worth its cost for anything the business can't afford to lose. A read replica costs the same way (another billed instance) but returns a very different benefit - it's not idle insurance, it's actively serving read traffic every day, directly reducing load on the primary and improving performance you use continuously. So Multi-AZ buys availability you hope not to need, while a read replica buys performance capacity you use immediately - both cost roughly the same per instance, but you're buying two different things.$$),
('sa-y-086','awssaa','feynman',4,'4.3',$$Explain how Aurora Serverless v2's ACU-based billing avoids waste for an application with a strong daily on/off traffic pattern, such as an internal reporting tool used only during business hours.$$,$$Model answer: A fixed-size provisioned Aurora cluster has to be sized for the tool's peak business-hours load, and that same capacity keeps being billed at night and on weekends when almost nobody is querying it - most of the paid-for capacity sits idle most of the week. Aurora Serverless v2 instead bills by the ACU-hour and continuously adjusts the number of ACUs to match real-time demand: as usage ramps up in the morning it scales up smoothly, and as usage drops off after hours it scales back down toward the configured minimum (which can be very low), so the bill shrinks right along with the idle time instead of staying pinned at the peak-sized rate around the clock.$$),
('sa-f-530','awssaa','core',4,'4.4',$$How do the costs of a Gateway VPC endpoint, an Interface VPC endpoint, and a NAT Gateway compare for reaching AWS services privately?$$,$$A Gateway VPC endpoint (S3 and DynamoDB only) has no hourly charge and no per-GB data processing charge - it's free. An Interface VPC endpoint (AWS PrivateLink, for most other AWS services) has both an hourly charge and a per-GB data processing charge, but is typically cheaper than routing the same traffic through a NAT Gateway, which also charges hourly plus per-GB and is usually the most expensive of the three for reaching AWS public endpoints.$$),
('sa-f-531','awssaa','core',4,'4.4',$$What is the AWS data transfer cost hierarchy, from free to most expensive?$$,$$Data transferred into AWS from the internet is free; traffic between resources in the same Availability Zone over private IPs is free; traffic between Availability Zones in the same region incurs a small per-GB charge each direction; traffic to another AWS region costs more per GB; and data transferred out to the public internet is billed at tiered, per-GB rates that usually make up the largest external network cost.$$),
('sa-f-532','awssaa','core',4,'4.4',$$How can a self-managed NAT instance be cheaper than a managed NAT Gateway?$$,$$A NAT instance is billed only as a normal EC2 instance, with no separate per-GB data-processing charge the way NAT Gateway has - at low to moderate traffic volumes this can cost less, at the trade-off of having to manage its patching, sizing, and failover yourself instead of getting NAT Gateway's built-in high availability and scaling.$$),
('sa-f-533','awssaa','core',4,'4.4',$$How does the cost model of VPC Peering compare to Transit Gateway for connecting VPCs?$$,$$VPC Peering has no hourly connection charge at all, only standard data transfer charges - cheapest for a small number of point-to-point connections. Transit Gateway charges per attachment-hour plus per-GB processed, but centralizes routing through one hub, which becomes more cost- and management-efficient than an ever-growing peering mesh once you're connecting many VPCs, since peering isn't transitive and full connectivity needs a connection per pair.$$),
('sa-f-534','awssaa','core',4,'4.4',$$What is the basic cost/setup trade-off between AWS Direct Connect and a Site-to-Site VPN?$$,$$A Site-to-Site VPN has low fixed cost (just a per-connection-hour charge) and can be provisioned in minutes, making it attractive for short-term needs or modest volume. Direct Connect charges a port-hour fee (plus usually a third-party cross-connect fee) and takes longer to provision, but offers dedicated bandwidth and, at sustained high data volumes, lower per-GB data transfer pricing that can make it cheaper overall despite the higher fixed cost.$$),
('sa-f-535','awssaa','core',4,'4.4',$$Why can migrating a high-volume, steady hybrid data transfer from VPN to Direct Connect reduce cost, independent of any latency benefit?$$,$$Direct Connect's per-GB data transfer OUT pricing is generally lower than standard internet-routed data transfer OUT pricing used by a VPN tunnel; at large, sustained daily transfer volumes, that lower per-GB rate outweighs Direct Connect's port-hour and connection fees, cutting the total bill even if latency were not a concern at all.$$),
('sa-f-536','awssaa','core',4,'4.4',$$How does putting Amazon CloudFront in front of S3 or an application reduce data-transfer cost, not just latency?$$,$$CloudFront's data transfer OUT pricing to end users is generally lower per GB than serving the same bytes directly from S3 or EC2 to the internet, and caching at the edge means repeat requests for the same object never generate a new data-transfer charge from the origin at all - both effects lower the network bill.$$),
('sa-f-537','awssaa','core',4,'4.4',$$Is S3 Transfer Acceleration a cost-free way to speed up uploads?$$,$$No - it adds its own per-GB fee on top of standard S3 transfer pricing. It's worth that extra cost mainly for large files moving long geographic distances (e.g., cross-continent uploads) where the speed and reliability gain justifies the fee; for small or already-nearby transfers it just adds cost with little benefit.$$),
('sa-f-538','awssaa','core',4,'4.4',$$What happens to the bill for an Elastic IP address that isn't attached to any running resource?$$,$$An unattached (or attached to a stopped instance) Elastic IP accrues an hourly charge until it's released or reattached to a running resource - Elastic IPs are only free while actively attached to a running instance.$$),
('sa-f-539','awssaa','core',4,'4.4',$$Does an idle load balancer with no traffic still cost money?$$,$$Yes - a load balancer bills its hourly charge regardless of how much traffic it actually processes, so a load balancer left running behind a decommissioned or unused application keeps generating cost until it's deleted.$$),
('sa-f-540','awssaa','core',4,'4.4',$$What changed about public IPv4 address pricing, and how can you reduce that cost?$$,$$AWS now charges an hourly fee for every public IPv4 address in use (including Elastic IPs and auto-assigned public IPs), not just unattached ones. Reducing the number of individually publicly-addressed resources - putting instances behind a NAT Gateway or load balancer instead of giving each one its own public IP, or adopting IPv6 where supported - directly lowers this cost.$$),
('sa-f-541','awssaa','core',4,'4.4',$$For a private subnet that needs outbound access to both S3 and a service without Gateway endpoint support (like SNS), what is the most cost-effective endpoint strategy?$$,$$Use a Gateway VPC endpoint for S3 traffic (free) and an Interface VPC endpoint (PrivateLink) for the service that doesn't support Gateway endpoints - routing either type of traffic through a NAT Gateway instead would add unnecessary hourly and per-GB charges.$$),
('sa-f-542','awssaa','core',4,'4.4',$$Why is running a Site-to-Site VPN as a Direct Connect backup usually cheaper than a second redundant Direct Connect connection?$$,$$A backup VPN only costs its low per-connection-hour rate and activates just during a Direct Connect outage, while a second Direct Connect connection means paying full port-hour (and possibly cross-connect) charges continuously for capacity that, most of the time, sits unused as pure redundancy.$$),
('sa-f-543','awssaa','core',4,'4.4',$$What is the general architectural principle for minimizing AWS data-transfer cost?$$,$$Keep frequently-communicating resources in the same Availability Zone (free) or at least the same region where possible, use Gateway/Interface VPC endpoints instead of NAT Gateway for AWS-service traffic, and use caching (like CloudFront) to reduce how often bytes have to cross a billed boundary at all - the fewer billed hops data crosses, the lower the transfer bill.$$),
('sa-a-169','awssaa','acronym',4,'4.4',$$VPCE$$,$$VPC Endpoint - a private connection from a VPC to a supported AWS service; Gateway endpoints (S3/DynamoDB) are free, Interface endpoints (PrivateLink, most other services) carry an hourly plus per-GB charge.$$),
('sa-a-170','awssaa','acronym',4,'4.4',$$NAT$$,$$Network Address Translation - the function a NAT Gateway (or self-managed NAT instance) performs so private-subnet resources can reach the internet outbound; NAT Gateway bills hourly plus per-GB processed.$$),
('sa-a-171','awssaa','acronym',4,'4.4',$$TGW$$,$$Transit Gateway - a central hub for connecting many VPCs and on-premises networks, billed per attachment-hour plus per-GB, more cost-efficient than a full VPC Peering mesh at scale.$$),
('sa-a-172','awssaa','acronym',4,'4.4',$$VIF$$,$$Virtual Interface - a logical Direct Connect connection (public, private, or transit) that determines what it can reach; choosing the wrong VIF type is a common reason a Direct Connect cost/architecture design fails to work as intended.$$),
('sa-y-087','awssaa','feynman',4,'4.4',$$Explain, from a pure cost perspective, how to choose between a Gateway VPC endpoint, an Interface VPC endpoint, and a NAT Gateway for private access to an AWS service.$$,$$Model answer: Start by asking which AWS service you're reaching - only S3 and DynamoDB support Gateway VPC endpoints, and those are free with no hourly or per-GB charge, so if the destination is one of those two, that's almost always the cheapest answer with no trade-off. For any other AWS service that supports PrivateLink, an Interface VPC endpoint costs an hourly fee plus a per-GB data-processing charge - not free, but generally still cheaper than the alternative of routing that traffic all the way out through a NAT Gateway, which charges its own hourly-plus-per-GB fee on top of being a shared resource for all outbound traffic. NAT Gateway is really the fallback: needed for traffic to destinations with no VPC endpoint support at all, such as general internet access, but the most expensive way to reach an AWS service that does support a private endpoint.$$),
('sa-y-088','awssaa','feynman',4,'4.4',$$Explain when Direct Connect is the cheaper choice versus a Site-to-Site VPN, since Direct Connect has higher fixed costs and a longer lead time.$$,$$Model answer: A VPN's cost is almost all variable and tiny to start - a low per-connection-hour rate and standard internet-routed data transfer pricing - so for a short-term need, a proof of concept, or genuinely modest and infrequent data volume, its low fixed cost and same-day setup make it the more economical and practical choice. Direct Connect flips that: you pay a recurring port-hour fee and often a physical cross-connect fee before moving a single byte, and provisioning can take weeks. That fixed cost only gets justified once the variable savings catch up - specifically, once you're moving a large, sustained volume of data every day, because Direct Connect's per-GB data transfer pricing is lower than internet-routed transfer. Below that volume threshold, VPN remains cheaper overall; above it, Direct Connect's lower per-GB rate eventually outweighs its higher fixed cost. The dedicated-bandwidth and lower-jitter benefits are a separate, non-cost reason to choose it even below that threshold, if the application truly needs them.$$),
('sa-y-089','awssaa','feynman',4,'4.4',$$Explain why putting CloudFront in front of an S3 bucket or application origin is often framed as a cost optimization rather than purely a performance one.$$,$$Model answer: Every byte an origin serves directly to the internet is billed at that origin's own data-transfer-out rate - for S3 or EC2 talking straight to end users worldwide, that adds up fast, especially for popular, frequently-requested content. CloudFront changes two things at once: first, its own data-transfer-out pricing to end users is generally lower per GB than the origin's direct internet transfer pricing, so even a cache miss that has to fetch from the origin and forward it typically costs less than the origin serving it directly. Second, and more importantly at scale, a cache hit is served entirely from the edge location and never touches the origin's data transfer bill at all - so the more repeat requests a popular object gets, the more of its total serving cost is eliminated rather than merely discounted. That's why teams that only cared about performance when they added CloudFront often find their S3/EC2 data-transfer line item drops sharply as a side effect.$$);
insert into public.questions (id, cert, domain, objective, qtype, difficulty, stem, choices, answer, explanation) values
('sa-q-500','awssaa',4,'4.1','mcq',1,$q$A team needs the cheapest possible S3 storage class for a secondary, easily-recreatable copy of data that already exists reliably elsewhere, and is comfortable with the risk of losing that copy if a single Availability Zone is destroyed. Which class fits?$q$,$q$["S3 Standard-IA","S3 One Zone-IA","S3 Standard","S3 Glacier Deep Archive"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$One Zone-IA stores data in only one AZ, costing roughly 20% less than Standard-IA, which is an acceptable trade-off only when the data is easily recreated or already durably stored elsewhere. Standard-IA and Standard cost more for the added resilience, and Deep Archive would add hours-long retrieval delay not implied here.$q$),
('sa-q-501','awssaa',4,'4.1','mcq',1,$q$A dataset is accessed roughly once every three months but must still be retrievable in milliseconds whenever it is needed. Which S3 class typically minimizes cost for this exact pattern?$q$,$q$["S3 Standard","S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval","S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval","S3 Standard-IA"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Glacier Instant Retrieval offers the same millisecond access as Standard-IA at an even lower storage price, trading a higher retrieval fee that's worth it once access is this infrequent. Standard costs more for no benefit here, and Flexible Retrieval doesn't offer millisecond access.$q$),
('sa-q-502','awssaa',4,'4.1','mcq',2,$q$An archived dataset in S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval must be restored within about an hour for an urgent compliance request, and the team is willing to pay extra for that speed. Which retrieval tier should they select?$q$,$q$["Bulk retrieval","Standard retrieval","Expedited retrieval","This is not possible with Glacier Flexible Retrieval"]$q$::jsonb,'2'::jsonb,$q$Expedited retrieval returns data in 1-5 minutes at the highest per-GB retrieval price, meeting an urgent within-the-hour need. Standard retrieval takes 3-5 hours and Bulk takes 5-12 hours - both too slow here, and Flexible Retrieval does support all three tiers.$q$),
('sa-q-503','awssaa',4,'4.1','mcq',2,$q$An object was transitioned to S3 Glacier Deep Archive and is then deleted only 60 days later. What is the billing consequence?$q$,$q$["No consequence; deletion is always free","The account is still charged for the remaining days up to Deep Archive's 180-day minimum storage duration","The deletion is free but future uploads are blocked for 180 days","S3 automatically restores the object instead of deleting it"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Glacier Deep Archive has a 180-day minimum storage duration charge; deleting an object at day 60 still bills for the remaining 120 days as if it had stayed stored. This is a real cost trap when lifecycle rules delete data too soon after archiving it.$q$),
('sa-q-504','awssaa',4,'4.1','mcq',3,$q$A team configures an S3 Lifecycle rule to transition objects into Standard-IA just 10 days after upload, expecting immediate savings. What is wrong with this plan?$q$,$q$["Nothing; the transition will save money starting immediately","S3 requires an object to be stored at least 30 days before it can transition into Standard-IA, so this rule as configured is not valid","Standard-IA has no minimum storage duration, so this is fine","Lifecycle transitions can only run once per year"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Objects must remain in S3 Standard for at least 30 days before a Lifecycle rule can transition them to Standard-IA (or One Zone-IA) - a rule attempting an earlier transition doesn't take effect as configured. This 30-day-first rule, combined with Standard-IA's own 30-day minimum storage charge, means transitioning short-lived objects rarely saves money anyway.$q$),
('sa-q-505','awssaa',4,'4.1','mcq',1,$q$A bucket accumulates storage cost from multipart uploads that were started but never completed due to client crashes and dropped connections. What Lifecycle rule directly addresses this waste?$q$,$q$["A rule to abort incomplete multipart uploads after a set number of days","A rule to enable Requester Pays","A rule to enable Intelligent-Tiering","A rule to enable Cross-Region Replication"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$An abort-incomplete-multipart-uploads rule automatically deletes orphaned upload parts after a configured number of days, reclaiming their storage cost. The other options address who pays for access, automatic tiering, and replication - none clean up abandoned upload parts.$q$),
('sa-q-506','awssaa',4,'4.1','multi',2,$q$Which of the following statements about what drives an S3 bill are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)$q$,$q$["PUT, COPY, POST, and LIST requests are priced higher per request than GET requests","Retrieving data from an IA or Glacier class incurs a data retrieval fee that Standard does not","Data transferred INTO S3 from the internet is billed the same as data transferred OUT","The total GB stored and how long it's stored both affect the storage portion of the bill"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$S3 bills more for write/list-style requests than reads, charges a retrieval fee for IA/Glacier reads that Standard doesn't have, and bills storage by GB-months (volume times duration). Data transfer IN to S3 is always free, unlike data transfer OUT, so the third statement is false.$q$),
('sa-q-507','awssaa',4,'4.1','mcq',1,$q$A team wants to lower their EBS bill for a fleet of gp2 volumes without reducing the baseline IOPS or throughput those volumes currently deliver. What is the most direct change?$q$,$q$["Switch the volumes to gp3, which includes the same or better baseline performance at a lower per-GB price","Switch the volumes to st1 Throughput Optimized HDD","Reduce the volume sizes to save money, accepting lower IOPS","Switch to S3 for the same workload"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$gp3 decouples baseline IOPS/throughput from volume size and prices lower per GB than gp2 for equivalent or better baseline performance. Switching to HDD or shrinking volumes would reduce performance, and S3 is object storage, not a drop-in block storage replacement.$q$),
('sa-q-508','awssaa',4,'4.1','mcq',2,$q$An account's EBS snapshot storage cost keeps growing every month because nightly snapshots are taken but never cleaned up. What combination of facts and fixes applies?$q$,$q$["Each snapshot after the first stores only changed blocks, and Amazon Data Lifecycle Manager (DLM) can automate deleting old snapshots on a retention schedule","Each snapshot is always a full independent copy, and there is no way to automate snapshot cleanup","Snapshots are always free regardless of count","Reducing the EBS volume size automatically deletes old snapshots"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Incremental snapshot billing already limits per-snapshot cost, but an unbounded retention still accumulates cost over time; DLM automates both snapshot creation and scheduled deletion so old snapshots don't pile up indefinitely. Snapshots are billed, and resizing a volume doesn't touch snapshot retention.$q$),
('sa-q-509','awssaa',4,'4.1','mcq',2,$q$An organization with dozens of AWS accounts wants a single dashboard showing storage usage and activity trends across all of them, specifically to spot buckets full of non-current object versions and incomplete multipart uploads wasting money. What should they use?$q$,$q$["AWS Trusted Advisor alone","Amazon S3 Storage Lens","AWS Cost Explorer alone","Amazon CloudWatch billing alarms"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$S3 Storage Lens is purpose-built for organization-wide storage usage and activity analytics, explicitly surfacing waste like non-current versions and incomplete multipart uploads across every account and bucket. Trusted Advisor and Cost Explorer give broader, less storage-specific detail, and billing alarms only alert on totals.$q$),
('sa-q-510','awssaa',4,'4.1','matching',2,$q$Match each storage cost-optimization tool or feature to what it actually does.$q$,$q${"left":["S3 Storage Lens","S3 Lifecycle rule to abort incomplete multipart uploads","EFS Lifecycle Management","Amazon Data Lifecycle Manager (DLM)"],"right":["Provides organization-wide dashboards on storage usage and activity to find wasted spend","Deletes orphaned parts of multipart uploads that were never completed","Automatically moves files unused for a configured period into EFS-IA","Automates the creation and scheduled deletion of EBS snapshots"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$Storage Lens gives account-wide storage analytics; the multipart-abort rule cleans up orphaned upload parts; EFS Lifecycle Management shifts cold files to the cheaper EFS-IA class; and DLM automates snapshot lifecycle so EBS snapshot cost doesn't grow unchecked.$q$),
('sa-q-511','awssaa',4,'4.1','ordering',2,$q$Order these S3/Glacier storage options from CHEAPEST to MOST EXPENSIVE typical price per GB-month.$q$,$q$["S3 Glacier Deep Archive","S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval","S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval","S3 Standard-IA","S3 Standard"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3,4]$q$::jsonb,$q$Deep Archive is the cheapest per-GB class, built for rarely-needed archives with hours-long retrieval; Glacier Flexible Retrieval costs a bit more for faster (minutes-to-hours) access; Glacier Instant Retrieval costs more still for millisecond access; Standard-IA costs more than that for no retrieval delay and a lower durability trade-off than Standard; and S3 Standard, with no retrieval fee or delay at all, is the most expensive per GB.$q$),
('sa-q-512','awssaa',4,'4.2','mcq',2,$q$A team needs a guarantee that 20 specific EC2 instances will be available in a particular Availability Zone for a product launch next month, and separately wants to minimize the cost of that guaranteed capacity once it keeps running long-term after launch. What should they do?$q$,$q$["Buy only a Compute Savings Plan and hope enough capacity is available","Create an On-Demand Capacity Reservation for the launch, then apply a Savings Plan to the reserved capacity for the ongoing discount","Rely on Spot Instances for the guaranteed launch capacity","Use a Dedicated Host, which is always the cheapest option regardless of use case"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A Capacity Reservation guarantees the capacity itself but charges full On-Demand pricing on its own; applying a Savings Plan on top adds the discount while keeping the guarantee. A Savings Plan alone doesn't guarantee capacity, Spot can be reclaimed, and a Dedicated Host isn't inherently the cheapest option.$q$),
('sa-q-513','awssaa',4,'4.2','mcq',1,$q$A company runs a fixed EC2 fleet in one instance family and one region for the next three years and wants the maximum possible compute discount, accepting the loss of flexibility to change families or regions. Which commitment gives the bigger discount?$q$,$q$["A Compute Savings Plan","An EC2 Instance Savings Plan locked to that family and region","On-Demand pricing","A Spot Fleet"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$An EC2 Instance Savings Plan can save up to roughly 72%, more than a Compute Savings Plan's roughly 66% cap, in exchange for locking in a specific family and region - exactly the trade the scenario describes. On-Demand and Spot don't fit a stable, long-term, discount-maximizing commitment.$q$),
('sa-q-514','awssaa',4,'4.2','mcq',2,$q$A Spot-based batch-processing fleet keeps losing large chunks of capacity at once because it depends heavily on a single EC2 instance type in a single Availability Zone. What most directly reduces this interruption risk while keeping Spot's cost savings?$q$,$q$["Switch entirely to On-Demand Instances","Diversify the Spot request across multiple instance types, sizes, and AZs using a capacity-optimized allocation strategy","Request Spot capacity for only one instance type but across more AZs","Increase the bid price on the single instance type"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Diversifying across instance types, sizes, and AZs with a capacity-optimized allocation strategy spreads risk across many Spot pools instead of depending on one, keeping the savings while reducing correlated interruptions. Switching to On-Demand loses the savings entirely, and AZ diversity alone doesn't fix single-instance-type concentration.$q$),
('sa-q-515','awssaa',4,'4.2','mcq',1,$q$A fault-tolerant ECS batch task processes queued jobs and can tolerate being stopped and restarted at any point. Which Fargate option minimizes its compute cost?$q$,$q$["Fargate On-Demand","Fargate Spot","A Compute Savings Plan applied only to Fargate On-Demand pricing","EC2 Dedicated Hosts running the same containers"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Fargate Spot offers a steep discount versus Fargate On-Demand for interruptible tasks, mirroring EC2 Spot's savings model for serverless containers. On-Demand and Dedicated Hosts cost more for a workload that explicitly tolerates interruption.$q$),
('sa-q-516','awssaa',4,'4.2','mcq',3,$q$A CPU-bound Lambda function is allocated 512 MB of memory and takes 4 seconds per invocation. Raising its memory to roughly 1,769 MB, which proportionally increases its CPU power, drops its duration to 1 second per invocation. Compared to the original 512 MB configuration, what happens to the cost per invocation?$q$,$q$["Cost per invocation increases, since the GB-second price is proportional to memory","Cost per invocation decreases, because the large drop in duration outweighs the higher memory allocation","Cost per invocation stays exactly the same regardless of memory or duration","Lambda does not allow increasing memory without a support ticket"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Total Lambda cost per invocation is memory times duration times the GB-second rate: 512 MB for 4 seconds bills roughly 2.0 GB-seconds, while about 1,769 MB for 1 second bills roughly 1.7 GB-seconds - fewer total GB-seconds despite the higher memory, so cost per invocation drops even though the per-second price rose.$q$),
('sa-q-517','awssaa',4,'4.2','mcq',2,$q$A media company must license per-CPU-socket third-party enterprise software on EC2 and needs visibility into and control over exactly which physical sockets and cores its instances run on. Which EC2 tenancy option fits?$q$,$q$["Dedicated Instances","Dedicated Hosts","Standard On-Demand instances","An EC2 Instance Savings Plan"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Dedicated Hosts are billed per physical server and expose the underlying sockets and cores, which is required for per-socket or per-core BYOL licensing. Dedicated Instances run on dedicated hardware too but don't expose host-level visibility, and standard On-Demand or a Savings Plan don't address dedicated tenancy at all.$q$),
('sa-q-518','awssaa',4,'4.2','multi',2,$q$Which of the following statements about Savings Plans versus Reserved Instances are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)$q$,$q$["Both bill for the committed amount for the full term regardless of actual usage","A Standard Reserved Instance can be resold on the RI Marketplace if it's no longer needed","A Savings Plan commitment can be resold on a marketplace the same way a Standard RI can","A Convertible Reserved Instance can be exchanged for a different instance family or OS as needs change"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$Both commitments are use-it-or-lose-it for their term, a Standard RI can be listed on the RI Marketplace if no longer needed, and a Convertible RI can be exchanged for different attributes - but Savings Plans have no resale marketplace, unlike Standard RIs.$q$),
('sa-q-519','awssaa',4,'4.2','matching',1,$q$Match each EC2/compute cost concept to what it actually does.$q$,$q${"left":["On-Demand Capacity Reservation","Fargate Spot","Dedicated Host","AWS Compute Optimizer (for Lambda)"],"right":["Guarantees capacity in an AZ with no billing discount by itself","Runs interruptible ECS/EKS Fargate tasks at a steep discount versus Fargate On-Demand","Bills per physical server, exposing sockets/cores for per-core software licensing","Recommends a Lambda memory setting that minimizes total cost for a function's workload"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$Each concept addresses a distinct cost lever: capacity reservations guarantee availability without a discount, Fargate Spot discounts interruptible serverless containers, Dedicated Hosts enable per-core licensing, and Compute Optimizer can recommend a cost-minimizing Lambda memory setting.$q$),
('sa-q-520','awssaa',4,'4.3','mcq',1,$q$A team wants Aurora Serverless v2 to bill them only for the database capacity actually consumed at each moment, scaling continuously rather than in a few large discrete jumps. What is the unit Aurora Serverless v2 bills by?$q$,$q$["EC2 instance-hours","Aurora Capacity Unit-hours (ACU-hours)","DynamoDB Read/Write Capacity Units","A flat monthly database fee regardless of usage"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Aurora Serverless v2 bills per ACU-hour, scaling continuously in fine-grained increments within a configured minimum/maximum ACU range, so cost tracks actual usage rather than a fixed instance size or a flat fee.$q$),
('sa-q-521','awssaa',4,'4.3','mcq',2,$q$A company's production database runs on a fixed RDS instance class and engine that has been stable for two years and will clearly continue unchanged for the foreseeable future. What is the most direct way to cut its compute cost?$q$,$q$["Switch to On-Demand pricing, which is already the cheapest option available","Purchase a 1- or 3-year RDS Reserved Instance for that instance class","Enable Multi-AZ","Add a read replica"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A stable, predictable, long-lived instance class and engine is exactly the profile that benefits most from an RDS Reserved Instance's discount versus On-Demand. Multi-AZ and read replicas both add cost for availability or read scaling; they don't reduce it.$q$),
('sa-q-522','awssaa',4,'4.3','mcq',1,$q$A team enables Multi-AZ on a production RDS instance. How does this change their RDS compute bill, and why?$q$,$q$["It roughly doubles it, since the synchronous standby is a second full, separately billed instance","It has no effect on compute cost, only on storage cost","It reduces cost by splitting traffic across two instances","It only adds a small flat monthly fee regardless of instance size"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The Multi-AZ standby is a complete second instance of the same class, billed continuously even though it only serves traffic during a failover - effectively doubling instance-hour cost for the availability guarantee.$q$),
('sa-q-523','awssaa',4,'4.3','mcq',1,$q$A development team's RDS database only needs to run during business hours on weekdays, but is currently left running 24/7 at full On-Demand cost. What is the simplest way to cut its compute cost without deleting the database?$q$,$q$["Stop the RDS instance outside business hours, using an automated stop/start schedule, so compute isn't billed while stopped","Delete the instance every night and recreate it every morning","Enable Multi-AZ to reduce cost","Switch the database engine from MySQL to PostgreSQL"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A stopped RDS instance isn't billed for compute, only allocated storage, making a scheduled stop/start ideal for a business-hours-only dev database - note AWS auto-restarts an instance left stopped for a full 7 days, so the schedule needs to restart it before then anyway. Deleting and recreating daily risks data loss and setup overhead; Multi-AZ and engine choice don't address idle-hours cost.$q$),
('sa-q-524','awssaa',4,'4.3','mcq',1,$q$A stable internal analytics table receives a well-known, steady volume of reads and writes 24 hours a day, with almost no variation month to month. Which DynamoDB capacity approach minimizes its ongoing cost?$q$,$q$["On-Demand capacity mode","Provisioned capacity with Reserved Capacity purchased for the steady baseline","Provisioned capacity with no Auto Scaling and no Reserved Capacity","Switching to Aurora Serverless v2 instead of DynamoDB"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$For steady, predictable, high-volume traffic, Provisioned capacity with a Reserved Capacity commitment gives the lowest per-unit rate. On-Demand's per-request pricing costs more at sustained high volume, and plain Provisioned without Reserved Capacity leaves the discount unclaimed.$q$),
('sa-q-525','awssaa',4,'4.3','mcq',2,$q$A newly launched mobile app's DynamoDB table sees traffic that is impossible to forecast - it might do ten times normal volume for an hour if a promotion goes viral, then fall back to near zero. Which capacity mode avoids both throttling during the spike and paying for idle capacity the rest of the time?$q$,$q$["Provisioned capacity sized for the historical average","On-Demand capacity mode","Provisioned capacity with a 3-year Reserved Capacity commitment","A fixed WCU/RCU value with Auto Scaling disabled"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$On-Demand mode charges per request and scales instantly with no capacity planning, matching genuinely unpredictable spiky traffic without either throttling or paying for unused headroom. The other options all require forecasting a load that, by the scenario's own description, cannot be forecast.$q$),
('sa-q-526','awssaa',4,'4.3','mcq',2,$q$A team runs a Provisioned-mode DynamoDB table whose throughput need has been flat and predictable for over a year, and wants to lower its per-unit RCU/WCU price without switching capacity mode. What should they purchase?$q$,$q$["DynamoDB Reserved Capacity for a 1- or 3-year term","An RDS Reserved Instance","EC2 Instance Savings Plans","S3 Requester Pays"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$DynamoDB Reserved Capacity is a 1- or 3-year commitment to a fixed amount of Provisioned throughput at a discounted rate, exactly matching a flat, predictable Provisioned-mode table. The other options apply to RDS, EC2, and S3, not DynamoDB throughput.$q$),
('sa-q-527','awssaa',4,'4.3','mcq',3,$q$A DynamoDB table has a predictable overnight-low, daytime-high traffic pattern. The team wants the discount of a capacity commitment for the predictable baseline, while still automatically absorbing the daytime increase without manual intervention. What combination achieves this?$q$,$q$["On-Demand capacity mode alone","Reserved Capacity sized to the overnight baseline, plus Auto Scaling to track the daytime increase","A single fixed Provisioned capacity value with no scaling","Aurora Serverless v2 instead of DynamoDB"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Reserved Capacity locks in a discounted floor for the predictable low-traffic baseline, and Auto Scaling adjusts capacity above that floor to track the daytime increase automatically - capturing the discount on the predictable part without paying peak rates around the clock. On-Demand alone forgoes the Reserved Capacity discount, and a fixed value can't track the daily swing.$q$),
('sa-q-528','awssaa',4,'4.3','mcq',3,$q$A DynamoDB table stores billions of rarely-read archival records, and the monthly bill is dominated by GB stored rather than by request volume. Which change would most directly reduce this table's cost?$q$,$q$["Switching the table to the DynamoDB Standard-IA table class, which lowers per-GB storage price in exchange for a higher per-request price","Enabling DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX)","Adding a Global Secondary Index","Switching from Provisioned to On-Demand capacity mode"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$DynamoDB Standard-IA trades a higher per-request price for a lower per-GB storage price, which is the right trade when storage, not throughput, dominates the bill - exactly this scenario. DAX adds caching cost, a GSI adds cost, and On-Demand doesn't address the storage-dominated cost driver.$q$),
('sa-q-529','awssaa',4,'4.3','mcq',1,$q$A DynamoDB table accumulates session records that are only useful for 24 hours, and the team currently runs a nightly batch job that scans for and deletes expired items, consuming write capacity every night. What DynamoDB feature replaces this job at no extra cost?$q$,$q$["Global Tables","Time to Live (TTL)","DynamoDB Streams","Point-in-time recovery"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$TTL automatically deletes items past a set expiration timestamp for free, in the background, eliminating the need for a manual scan-and-delete job and the write capacity it consumed. Global Tables, Streams, and point-in-time recovery solve different problems.$q$),
('sa-q-530','awssaa',4,'4.3','mcq',2,$q$A single-region application with no international users is considering adopting Aurora Global Database purely because it sounds like a best practice. What cost consideration should make them reconsider?$q$,$q$["Global Database is free to enable and has no downside","Global Database bills for replicated storage plus at least one running reader instance in every secondary region - an ongoing cost that only pays off if the cross-region latency or DR benefit is actually needed","Global Database only works with DynamoDB, not Aurora","Global Database replaces the need for backups, so it saves money overall"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Global Database's secondary regions each cost ongoing money for replicated storage and a running reader instance; without an actual cross-region latency or DR requirement, that's pure added cost with no corresponding benefit for a single-region app.$q$),
('sa-q-531','awssaa',4,'4.3','mcq',3,$q$A team on Aurora Serverless v1 finds that traffic dips several times a day are not reflected in their bill, because capacity only steps down in large discrete increments and briefly pauses connections when it does. What migration would better match billed capacity to actual real-time load?$q$,$q$["Migrating to Aurora Serverless v2, which scales continuously in fine-grained increments without a connection-dropping pause","Migrating to a fixed-size provisioned Aurora instance","Enabling Multi-AZ on the v1 cluster","Switching to RDS for MySQL instead of Aurora"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Serverless v2's continuous, fine-grained ACU scaling tracks real-time load far more precisely than v1's coarse capacity steps, reducing the ACU-hours paid for but not used and avoiding v1's scaling-event connection pause. A fixed-size instance would be even less responsive to the dips, and Multi-AZ or a different engine don't address billing granularity.$q$),
('sa-q-532','awssaa',4,'4.3','mcq',2,$q$An RDS database's automated and manual snapshots have grown well beyond the size of the database's own allocated storage, and the monthly bill now includes new backup storage charges. What explains this?$q$,$q$["RDS provides free backup storage only up to the size of the database's total allocated storage; snapshot storage beyond that allowance is billed per GB-month","RDS backups are never free at any size","This charge is unrelated to snapshots and must be a compute cost increase","Automated backups are billed but manual snapshots are always free"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$RDS backup storage is free only up to the size of the database's allocated storage; once cumulative snapshots exceed that allowance, the excess is billed per GB-month, which is why pruning unneeded manual snapshots keeps this cost in check.$q$),
('sa-q-533','awssaa',4,'4.3','mcq',1,$q$A team added an RDS read replica six months ago for a reporting workload that was retired three months ago, but the replica is still running. What is the direct cost consequence?$q$,$q$["None - RDS read replicas are billed only when actively queried","The replica continues to be billed as a full, separate running instance whether or not anything reads from it","The replica automatically stops itself if unused for 30 days","Read replicas share the primary's billed instance-hours, adding no extra cost"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A read replica is its own fully billed running instance, independent of how much (or how little) read traffic it actually serves - an idle, forgotten replica keeps costing money until someone deletes it.$q$),
('sa-q-534','awssaa',4,'4.3','mcq',3,$q$A read-heavy RDS application repeatedly re-runs the same handful of expensive queries, and the team is deciding between adding another RDS read replica or adding an ElastiCache layer in front of the primary. Purely from a cost-efficiency standpoint, which typically wins for this specific pattern, and why?$q$,$q$["Another read replica, because it is always cheaper than any caching layer","ElastiCache, because a relatively small in-memory cache can absorb repeated identical queries far more cheaply than paying for an entire additional database instance","Neither reduces cost; only Reserved Instances reduce RDS cost","A read replica, because ElastiCache cannot be used together with RDS"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$When the same queries repeat, a modestly sized cache can absorb most of that read load for far less than the cost of an entire additional database instance sized to hold the whole dataset - a full read replica is a more expensive way to solve a problem that's really about repeated, cacheable reads.$q$),
('sa-q-535','awssaa',4,'4.3','multi',2,$q$Which of the following statements about combining commitment-based discounts with on-demand database capacity are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)$q$,$q$["Reserved Capacity/Reserved Instances are most cost-effective when sized to a workload's steady, predictable baseline","On-Demand (or Auto Scaling above a reserved floor) is well suited to absorbing the unpredictable or bursty portion of demand","Committing 100% of a variable workload's peak capacity to a 3-year Reserved Instance is generally the most cost-efficient approach","DynamoDB Reserved Capacity can be applied to On-Demand-mode tables to reduce their per-request price"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$Sizing a commitment to the steady baseline and letting flexible capacity absorb bursts is the cost-efficient pattern; committing peak capacity for years wastes money during every non-peak period, and Reserved Capacity only applies to Provisioned-mode DynamoDB tables, not On-Demand ones.$q$),
('sa-q-536','awssaa',4,'4.3','ordering',1,$q$Order these RDS pricing commitments from SHORTEST commitment (most flexible, least discount) to LONGEST commitment (least flexible, biggest discount).$q$,$q$["On-Demand (no term commitment)","1-year Reserved Instance","3-year Reserved Instance"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2]$q$::jsonb,$q$On-Demand requires no commitment and offers no discount; a 1-year RI trades some flexibility for a moderate discount; a 3-year RI locks in the longest term for the deepest discount - discount size scales with commitment length.$q$),
('sa-q-537','awssaa',4,'4.3','matching',2,$q$Match each database cost-optimization concept to what it actually does.$q$,$q${"left":["Aurora Capacity Unit (ACU)","DynamoDB Standard-IA table class","Stopping an RDS instance outside business hours","Aurora Global Database's ongoing cost"],"right":["The per-hour billing/scaling unit for Aurora Serverless v2 capacity","Lowers per-GB storage price in exchange for a higher per-request price, for storage-dominated tables","Avoids compute billing while stopped, though storage is still billed and AWS auto-restarts after 7 days","Bills for replicated storage plus a running reader instance in every secondary region, regardless of whether cross-region access is needed"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$Each concept is a distinct database cost lever: the ACU is Serverless v2's billing unit, Standard-IA trades request price for storage price, stopping an instance pauses compute billing (with a 7-day auto-restart caveat), and Global Database's secondary regions add continuous cost regardless of whether they're actually needed.$q$),
('sa-q-538','awssaa',4,'4.4','mcq',1,$q$Private EC2 instances need to call the AWS Systems Manager API without traversing the public internet or a NAT Gateway. Systems Manager does not support a Gateway VPC endpoint. What is the most cost-effective private-access option?$q$,$q$["Add another NAT Gateway dedicated to Systems Manager traffic","Create an Interface VPC endpoint (AWS PrivateLink) for Systems Manager","Route the traffic over the public internet instead","Do nothing; a NAT Gateway is required for all AWS API calls"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Since Systems Manager has no Gateway endpoint option, an Interface VPC endpoint is the private-access path - it isn't free like a Gateway endpoint, but it's generally cheaper than routing the same traffic through a NAT Gateway. Adding another NAT Gateway or going over the public internet would cost more or reintroduce exposure.$q$),
('sa-q-539','awssaa',4,'4.4','mcq',1,$q$Which of these VPC connectivity options has NO hourly charge and NO per-GB data processing charge?$q$,$q$["Interface VPC endpoint (PrivateLink)","Gateway VPC endpoint (S3 and DynamoDB only)","NAT Gateway","Transit Gateway attachment"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A Gateway VPC endpoint, available only for S3 and DynamoDB, is entirely free - no hourly or per-GB charge. Interface endpoints, NAT Gateways, and Transit Gateway attachments all carry both an hourly charge and a per-GB data processing charge.$q$),
('sa-q-540','awssaa',4,'4.4','multi',2,$q$Which of the following statements about AWS data transfer pricing are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)$q$,$q$["Data transferred into AWS from the internet is always free","Data transferred between two instances in the same Availability Zone over private IPs is free","Data transferred between Availability Zones in the same region is free, exactly like same-AZ traffic","Data transferred out to the internet is billed at tiered, decreasing per-GB rates as volume grows"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$Data transfer IN is always free, same-AZ private-IP traffic is free, and internet-bound data transfer OUT uses tiered, decreasing per-GB pricing. Cross-AZ traffic in the same region is NOT free - it incurs a small per-GB charge each direction, unlike same-AZ traffic.$q$),
('sa-q-541','awssaa',4,'4.4','mcq',1,$q$A startup with lightweight, infrequent outbound internet traffic from a private subnet wants to minimize network cost and is comfortable managing an EC2 instance for it. What is a lower-cost alternative to a managed NAT Gateway at their traffic level?$q$,$q$["A NAT instance - a self-managed EC2 instance performing network address translation","A Transit Gateway","An Interface VPC endpoint","AWS Direct Connect"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A NAT instance is billed only as a normal EC2 instance with no separate per-GB processing charge, which can be cheaper than NAT Gateway at low-to-moderate volume, at the cost of self-managing its availability and scaling. Transit Gateway, Interface endpoints, and Direct Connect solve different connectivity problems.$q$),
('sa-q-542','awssaa',4,'4.4','mcq',2,$q$A company needs to connect exactly two VPCs so resources in each can reach the other directly, with no plan to add more VPCs. Which connectivity option minimizes cost for just this pairwise connection?$q$,$q$["VPC Peering, which has no hourly connection charge - only standard data transfer applies","AWS Transit Gateway, which charges per-attachment-hour plus per-GB regardless of VPC count","AWS Direct Connect","A NAT Gateway in each VPC"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$For just two VPCs, Peering's lack of any hourly connection charge makes it cheaper than Transit Gateway's per-attachment-hour fee; Transit Gateway's cost advantage only appears once many VPCs need centralized routing. Direct Connect and NAT Gateways solve on-premises and internet-egress connectivity, not VPC-to-VPC peering.$q$),
('sa-q-543','awssaa',4,'4.4','mcq',3,$q$A company connecting 15 VPCs to each other and to an on-premises network is deciding between a full mesh of VPC Peering connections and a single AWS Transit Gateway. From a cost and management perspective, why would Transit Gateway likely be preferred despite its per-attachment and per-GB charges?$q$,$q$["Transit Gateway is always free regardless of scale","A full peering mesh needs a rapidly growing number of individual connections as VPCs are added, since peering isn't transitive, while Transit Gateway centralizes routing through one hub with a linear, predictable per-attachment cost as VPCs are added","VPC Peering supports transitive routing, so a mesh isn't actually needed","Direct Connect is required to connect more than two VPCs"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Because VPC Peering connections aren't transitive, connecting N VPCs in a full mesh requires a connection for every pair - growing quadratically - while Transit Gateway scales linearly, one attachment per VPC, which becomes both cheaper to manage and often cheaper overall at this scale despite its per-attachment and per-GB charges.$q$),
('sa-q-544','awssaa',4,'4.4','mcq',2,$q$A finance company needs a hybrid network connection set up within a day or two for a short-term project with a modest, unpredictable data volume, and does not want a long procurement lead time or an ongoing port-hour commitment. Which option fits both the cost and timeline needs?$q$,$q$["AWS Direct Connect","AWS Site-to-Site VPN","A new physical cross-connect at a colocation facility","A Transit Gateway alone, with no on-premises connection"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A Site-to-Site VPN has low fixed cost and can be stood up in minutes to hours, fitting a short-term, modest-volume, time-sensitive need. Direct Connect and a physical cross-connect both involve port-hour or facility fees and multi-week lead times, and Transit Gateway alone doesn't provide the on-premises link needed.$q$),
('sa-q-545','awssaa',4,'4.4','mcq',3,$q$A manufacturing company transfers many terabytes per day between its data center and AWS every single day, year-round, over a Site-to-Site VPN routed across the public internet. Purely on data-transfer cost grounds, ignoring latency, why might migrating this to AWS Direct Connect reduce their bill?$q$,$q$["Direct Connect data transfer OUT is generally priced lower per GB than standard internet-routed data transfer OUT, and at sustained high volume that per-GB saving outweighs Direct Connect's port-hour and connection fees","Direct Connect makes all data transfer completely free","VPN data transfer is always more expensive than Direct Connect regardless of volume","Direct Connect eliminates the need to pay for any AWS compute or storage"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Direct Connect's lower per-GB data transfer OUT pricing only becomes cost-effective once its fixed port-hour and connection fees are outweighed by the savings on a large, sustained transfer volume - exactly this company's daily terabyte-scale pattern. It doesn't make transfer free, and the VPN-vs-DX comparison depends on volume, not a blanket rule.$q$),
('sa-q-546','awssaa',4,'4.4','mcq',1,$q$Static content served directly from an S3 bucket to end users worldwide is generating a large data-transfer-out bill. Which change is likely to reduce that specific cost, in addition to improving latency?$q$,$q$["Enabling S3 Versioning","Serving the content through Amazon CloudFront instead of directly from S3","Switching the bucket to S3 One Zone-IA","Disabling S3 access logging"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$CloudFront's data transfer OUT pricing to end users is generally cheaper per GB than S3 serving directly to the internet, and edge caching means repeat requests never generate a new origin data-transfer charge at all. Versioning, storage class, and logging don't affect data-transfer-out pricing.$q$),
('sa-q-547','awssaa',4,'4.4','mcq',2,$q$A media company uploads large video files from offices on multiple continents into a single S3 bucket and finds standard uploads slow and inconsistent, but their upload volume is modest. Would enabling S3 Transfer Acceleration be a clear cost win here?$q$,$q$["Yes, Transfer Acceleration is always free and only improves things","No - Transfer Acceleration adds a per-GB fee on top of standard transfer pricing, so it should be reserved for genuinely large, long-distance transfers where the reliability and speed gain justifies the extra cost","Transfer Acceleration replaces the need for S3 storage costs entirely","Transfer Acceleration only affects downloads, not uploads"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Transfer Acceleration is not free; it adds its own per-GB charge, so it's worth adopting mainly for large files moving long geographic distances, not blanket-applied to modest-volume uploads where the extra cost may outweigh the benefit.$q$),
('sa-q-548','awssaa',4,'4.4','mcq',1,$q$An Elastic IP address was allocated for an instance that has since been terminated, and the address was never released. What is the cost consequence?$q$,$q$["No cost - Elastic IPs are always free","The unattached Elastic IP accrues an hourly charge until it is released or reattached to a running resource","It automatically converts to a public IPv4 address pool entry at no charge","AWS automatically deletes unattached Elastic IPs within an hour"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$An Elastic IP is free only while attached to a running resource; once unattached (or attached to a stopped instance), it accrues an hourly charge until released or reattached - a common source of forgotten, wasted spend.$q$),
('sa-q-549','awssaa',4,'4.4','mcq',2,$q$A cost review finds a load balancer that has received zero traffic for the past four months, sitting behind a decommissioned application. What is the cost impact of leaving it running?$q$,$q$["None - load balancers only bill for traffic actually processed","It continues to accrue its hourly charge regardless of how little or no traffic it processes, until it is deleted","It automatically stops billing after 30 days of no traffic","Load balancers are billed once at creation, with no ongoing charge"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A load balancer bills its hourly charge regardless of traffic volume, so an idle load balancer behind a retired application keeps costing money indefinitely until someone deletes it.$q$),
('sa-q-550','awssaa',4,'4.4','mcq',2,$q$Since AWS began charging for public IPv4 addresses, what is a direct way to reduce that specific cost for a fleet of EC2 instances that don't strictly need individually addressable public IPs?$q$,$q$["Give every instance an additional Elastic IP for redundancy","Move instances to private IP addressing behind a NAT Gateway or load balancer instead of assigning each one its own public IPv4 address, or adopt IPv6 where possible","Enable Multi-AZ on every instance","Switch every instance to Dedicated Hosts"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Since every public IPv4 address now accrues an hourly charge, reducing how many resources hold their own individually-addressable public IP - by centralizing outbound/inbound access through a NAT Gateway or load balancer, or using IPv6 - directly lowers this specific cost. Adding more Elastic IPs, Multi-AZ, or Dedicated Hosts don't address public IPv4 cost.$q$),
('sa-q-551','awssaa',4,'4.4','matching',2,$q$Match each VPC connectivity/access method to its typical cost characteristic.$q$,$q${"left":["Gateway VPC endpoint (S3/DynamoDB)","Interface VPC endpoint (PrivateLink)","NAT Gateway","VPC Peering"],"right":["No hourly charge and no per-GB data processing charge","Hourly charge plus per-GB data processing charge, needed for AWS services without Gateway endpoint support","Hourly charge plus per-GB data processing charge, typically the most expensive way to reach AWS public service endpoints from a private subnet","No hourly connection charge; only standard data transfer charges apply between the peered VPCs"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$Each option has a distinct cost profile: Gateway endpoints are entirely free, Interface endpoints cost money but fill the gap for services without Gateway support, NAT Gateway is usually the priciest path to AWS public endpoints, and Peering has no hourly fee at all, just standard data transfer.$q$),
('sa-q-552','awssaa',4,'4.4','multi',2,$q$Which of the following statements about combining AWS Direct Connect with a Site-to-Site VPN are TRUE? (Select all that apply.)$q$,$q$["A VPN can serve as a low-fixed-cost encrypted backup path if the primary Direct Connect link fails","Direct Connect traffic itself is encrypted by default, the same as a VPN tunnel","Running a VPN as a Direct Connect backup is typically far cheaper than provisioning and paying for a second redundant Direct Connect connection","Direct Connect and VPN cannot both terminate on the same virtual private gateway"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,2]$q$::jsonb,$q$A VPN backup path is a low-cost way to add resilience to a Direct Connect link, and it's typically far cheaper than a second full Direct Connect connection kept mostly idle as redundancy. Direct Connect traffic is NOT encrypted by default, unlike a VPN tunnel, and both can terminate on the same virtual private gateway for exactly this backup pattern.$q$),
('sa-q-553','awssaa',4,'4.4','multi',2,$q$A team is reviewing their monthly AWS bill for unnecessary network spend. Which of the following would directly reduce cost if changed? (Select all that apply.)$q$,$q$["Releasing Elastic IP addresses that are no longer attached to any running resource","Deleting load balancers that no longer serve any active application","Moving S3-bound traffic from private subnets off a NAT Gateway and onto a Gateway VPC endpoint","Increasing the number of NAT Gateways per Availability Zone without changing traffic patterns"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2]$q$::jsonb,$q$Releasing unused Elastic IPs, deleting idle load balancers, and moving S3 traffic onto a free Gateway endpoint each directly cut cost. Adding more NAT Gateways without a traffic-pattern reason only adds more hourly charges for no benefit.$q$),
('sa-q-554','awssaa',4,'4.4','ordering',1,$q$Order these ways of reaching Amazon S3 from a private subnet from MOST expensive to LEAST expensive in typical per-GB data-processing cost, assuming an identical traffic pattern.$q$,$q$["Routing through a NAT Gateway to reach S3's public endpoint","Routing through an Interface VPC endpoint for S3","Routing through a Gateway VPC endpoint for S3"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2]$q$::jsonb,$q$A NAT Gateway charges both an hourly fee and a per-GB processing fee and is generally the most expensive path to a public AWS endpoint; an Interface VPC endpoint also charges hourly plus per-GB but typically costs less than NAT for the same traffic; a Gateway VPC endpoint, available for S3, has no hourly or per-GB charge at all, making it the cheapest.$q$),
('sa-q-555','awssaa',4,'4.4','ordering',2,$q$Order these hybrid/inter-VPC connectivity options from LOWEST typical fixed/hourly cost to HIGHEST typical fixed/hourly cost.$q$,$q$["VPC Peering (no hourly connection charge)","AWS Site-to-Site VPN (billed per connection-hour)","AWS Direct Connect (port-hour plus possible cross-connect fees)"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2]$q$::jsonb,$q$VPC Peering has no hourly connection charge at all; a Site-to-Site VPN adds a modest per-connection-hour fee; Direct Connect carries the highest fixed cost, with a port-hour charge and often a third-party cross-connect fee, in exchange for dedicated bandwidth and lower variable transfer pricing at scale.$q$);
